Z||000000||Newbie||10-19-2000||08:06 PM||||  Man am I hooked! I bought an 84 SE Coupe that is loaded, but the Duke is siezed along with the steering column.<BR>  I just got her home and a friend Fiero owner says it take 2 people to fix the problem. One to rock the car and the other to wiggle the key and steering to get it free. I haven't seen anything in the posts about this. One night it was fine and the next it sucked. I personally thing the igition key lock is hosed, because I get a solid tone if I push the key in all the way and the chime if I just wiggle it. I can wiggle the steering wheel and the key, so I no nothing is in a hard bind. Does any body have a suggestion?<BR> This little baby needs a lot of TLC and the stuff I have been reading is fantasic. Already several fo my questions have been answered. I am up to 60 days on the tech Questions and haven't even touched the Archives. This place is great, what a wealth of information, and I have just begun.<BR> I just sit in MY "new Toy" and know things are going to be great once I get her on the road. Definately a new power plant, but where to go? Stay with the Duke, for economy or go to the 6 conversion. I drive 44 miles one way to work so I would like to keep around 20 MPG, Any suggestions?<BR> I have never ridden in a Duke powered Fiero, or any Fiero, for that fact. I have heard the Duke is under powered. I don't want to jerk my head back, YET, when I stomp it, but I don't want to get run over entering the freeway, or passed by Semi's in the mountains either. Believe me I know the feeling. My regular ride is a 4 popper ranger, and I feel so ungratified! All you good people out there, bear with a new post and his enthusiasm. Can you put me on the right path? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-19-2000||09:02 PM||||I don't know about the steering column lock, sounds like time for replacement.  See Pontiac.  If the Fiero duke is a manual transmission, it won't snap your neck upon take off, but should perform quite well for the commute.  I can get 30+ mpg with my 2.8 GT over the road, so if you drive sanely, you should get at least that or more with the duke.<P>I had a chance to drive an 84 4 speed for a day.  I thought the car ran well and really liked to rev up to red line. Semed like a fun car.  I couldn't tell the mpg, the odometer didn't work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||10-19-2000||09:05 PM||||Just a guesss, but you might check for a maladjusted ignition switch.  The ignition switch is a rectangular ceramic box mounted on top of the steering column, operated by a link rod to the lock cylinder.  If it is not screwed down in the right spot, it can make the key difficult to remove (like mine).  Might not be your problem, but it's a thought.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||10-19-2000||09:36 PM||||Well first off, Welcome to the Forum.<P>I had two duke Fieros and yes they're knd of slow off the line but they're fun on the highway. I raced a T/A with one of mine and he couldn't loose me, actually at one point I actually started pulling up on him.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||10-19-2000||10:06 PM||||A manual tranny doesn't have as much snap as an auto.. unless you like replacing clutches alot.  My '84 auto feels like it launches harder then my GT, even when I launch it at 3,000.  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||10-20-2000||12:20 AM||||If its an automatic, make sure it is all the way in park.||||reg||1
